ntfs-macos patch 0003: accept an inherited descriptor "/dev/fd/N" as the device (macOS only)

Rationale (T-M2-05, U5, docs/spikes/M2-fdpass.md): the daemon (root) opens the NTFS device and
starts ntfs-3g as the console user with that descriptor inherited, named /dev/fd/N. The user has
no permission on /dev/diskN (root:operator 0640). Unpatched NTFS-3G 2026.9.28 fails three ways
on macOS 27:
  1. realpath() of /dev/fd/N for a regular file returns "/dev/fd/<file name>" (e.g.
     /dev/fd/img0.img), which does not exist: "Failed to access '/dev/fd/img0.img'".
  2. main() refuses a block device for a non-root user with the external FUSE library
     (unpriv_fuseblk_msg, exit 19), before opening anything.
  3. unix_io.c open()s the name again; on macOS open("/dev/fd/N") by an unprivileged process
     fails with EACCES when the process has no permission on the underlying file, even though it
     holds N ("Error opening '/dev/fd/3': Permission denied").
Hunks:
  - include/ntfs-3g/device.h, libntfs-3g/unix_io.c: new exported ntfs_device_inherited_fd()
    returns N only for the exact name "/dev/fd/N" (decimal, no leading zero, N >= 3) when N is
    open in this process. unix_io open then uses fstat(N) instead of stat(name) and
    F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC of N instead of open(name), and marks N close-on-exec (N stays open: a retry
    duplicates it again, and closing any descriptor of the file would drop the POSIX lock).
    The access mode is never widened: a write-only N is refused, and a read/write request on an
    N that is not O_RDWR fails with EBADF. Not EROFS: ntfs_device_mount() (volume.c) retries
    read-only on any EROFS, which would silently turn a read/write request into a read-only
    mount. O_EXCL is not added (meaningless for a duplicate). The advisory lock is still taken
    on the duplicate; it gives no lasting exclusivity (ntfs-3g calls daemon() after ntfs_open(),
    and POSIX locks do not survive the fork). Exclusivity of the device comes from the caller
    (ntfs-macos daemon: ADR-020 binding and the Disk Arbitration dissenter).
  - src/ntfs-3g_common.c: keep the name "/dev/fd/N" as given instead of realpath().
  - src/ntfs-3g.c: skip the non-root block device refusal only when the device is such an
    inherited descriptor and fstat() of it is a block device. Any block device given by path is
    still refused for a non-root user.
Scope: the unix_io change is library-wide on macOS: every libntfs-3g user (ntfs-3g, ntfsfix,
mkntfs and the other ntfsprogs, ntfs-3g.probe, ntfsmac-probe, ntfsmac-volmark) accepts
"/dev/fd/N" this way, and the library exports one new symbol. lowntfs-3g gets the realpath
change through ntfs-3g_common.c but keeps its own block device refusal (lowntfs-3g.c, main),
so it still refuses inherited block devices; it is not patched and must not be shipped.
Safety: every change applies only to a descriptor the process already holds open; a descriptor
gives access the holder already has, so nothing here grants access to a device the process
could not already read or write through N. Paths other than "/dev/fd/N" behave as upstream.
All hunks are under __APPLE__; the Linux build (Environment A, internal FUSE) is unchanged.
Upstream status: not submitted (macOS packaging specific).
Test: I-SMOKE-02, tests/integration/smoke/fd_pass.sh (Environment B guest).

